20l1|n|Made wholly or in part of shoddy; containing shoddy; as, shoddy cloth; shoddy blankets; hence, colloquially, not genuine; sham; pretentious; as, shoddy aristocracy.	v:np0,18,1~np0,18,1	N:2h01,11,2~2h01,11,2
20l1|v|A fibrous material obtained by "deviling," or tearing into fibers, refuse woolen goods, old stockings, rags, druggets, etc. See Mungo. ; A fabric of inferior quality made of, or containing a large amount of, shoddy.	D:1w5c,23,1~1w5c,23,1
20l1|a|Made wholly or in part of shoddy; containing shoddy; as, shoddy cloth; shoddy blankets; hence, colloquially, not genuine; sham; pretentious; as, shoddy aristocracy.	j:1w5c,47,5;2h01,40,14~2h01,40,14;1w5c,47,5
20l6|n|A covering for the human foot, usually made of leather, having a thick and somewhat stiff sole and a lighter top. It differs from a boot on not extending so far up the leg. ; Anything resembling a shoe in form, position, or use. ; A plate or rim of iron nailed to the hoof of an animal to defend it from injury. ; A band of iron or steel, or a ship of wood, fastened to the bottom of the runner of a sleigh, or any vehicle which slides on the snow. ; A drag, or sliding piece of wood or iron, placed under the wheel of a loaded vehicle, to retard its motion in going down a hill. ; The part of a railroad car brake which presses upon the wheel to retard its motion. ; A trough-shaped or spout-shaped member, put at the bottom of the water leader coming from the eaves gutter, so as to throw the water off from the building. ; The trough or spout for conveying the grain from the hopper to the eye of the millstone. ; An inclined trough in an ore-crushing mill. ; An iron socket or plate to take the thrust of a strut or rafter. ; An iron socket to protect the point of a wooden pile. ; A plate, or notched piece, interposed between a moving part and the stationary part on which it bears, to take the wear and afford means of adjustment; -- called also slipper, and gib. ; To furnish with a shoe or shoes; to put a shoe or shoes on; as, to shoe a horse, a sled, an anchor. ; To protect or ornament with something which serves the purpose of a shoe; to tip.	N:21cl,66,5~21cl,66,5
20l6|a|A covering for the human foot, usually made of leather, having a thick and somewhat stiff sole and a lighter top. It differs from a boot on not extending so far up the leg. ; Anything resembling a shoe in form, position, or use. ; A plate or rim of iron nailed to the hoof of an animal to defend it from injury. ; A band of iron or steel, or a ship of wood, fastened to the bottom of the runner of a sleigh, or any vehicle which slides on the snow. ; A drag, or sliding piece of wood or iron, placed under the wheel of a loaded vehicle, to retard its motion in going down a hill. ; The part of a railroad car brake which presses upon the wheel to retard its motion. ; A trough-shaped or spout-shaped member, put at the bottom of the water leader coming from the eaves gutter, so as to throw the water off from the building. ; The trough or spout for conveying the grain from the hopper to the eye of the millstone. ; An inclined trough in an ore-crushing mill. ; An iron socket or plate to take the thrust of a strut or rafter. ; An iron socket to protect the point of a wooden pile. ; A plate, or notched piece, interposed between a moving part and the stationary part on which it bears, to take the wear and afford means of adjustment; -- called also slipper, and gib. ; To furnish with a shoe or shoes; to put a shoe or shoes on; as, to shoe a horse, a sled, an anchor. ; To protect or ornament with something which serves the purpose of a shoe; to tip.	j:21cl,43,1~21cl,43,1
